{
  "abstract": "Hajós joins of two copies of the join of a six-clique and a pentagon provide nine-chromatic starting graphs for the Earth–Moon problem. We prove that every biplanar spanning supergraph of any of the ten first-generation constructors is nine-chromatic. All extra edges on the original 21 vertices are allowed, including restorations of deleted edges and chords within either parent. The proof matches the two private eight-color palettes. Euler’s bound limits the possible Hall obstructions, and the required contacts at low-degree rim vertices allow one independent color class to split when a matching fails. We also prove that two intact copies meeting at one vertex suffice on 21 vertices. For a labeled 99-edge seed, we then allow the old graph to range up to an explicit 121-edge template containing a published pair of triangulations. Every biplanar extension by two vertices is nine-chromatic, with arbitrary neighborhoods and either choice of adjacency. For a new triangle, at least 106 retained old edges suffice, as do two new vertices of degree nine at any degree of the third. Positive coloring words and two executable reductions establish the finite parts. The results exclude these augmentations as sources of ten colors while leaving other templates and larger constructions open.",
